  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1127 AM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nHigh temperatures will continue to run 5-10 degrees above average\ntoday as the upper-level ridge persists above southern New Mexico.\nSoutheasterly flow surrounding the high will bring more moisture\ninto the region. The mountains will have the best chance (40-60%)\nof scattered thunderstorms, but the lowlands will have at least a\nmentionable chance (15-25%) of isolated thunderstorms this\nafternoon as PWAT values for the lowlands are between 1-1.2\" based\non the latest GFS and ECMWF runs. Storm-generated outflow gusts up\nto 50 mph are possible in the lowlands this evening and into the \novernight hours.\n\nThe latest mesoscale model runs convinced me to put a blanket\nmentionable precipitation chance area-wide as PWAT values on\nFriday continue to hover around 1.2\", in addition to the WPC\nhaving our region under a marginal Excessive Rainfall Outlook for\nboth today and Friday. I expect thunderstorms to mainly be \nrelegated to the mountains, though a spotty lowland shower or \nthunderstorm may form Friday afternoon and evening, especially in \nthe Gila National Forest and areas around or just west of the \nContinental Divide.\n\nAs the ridge begins to move east, temperatures will start to cool\ndown to just under the triple digit mark this weekend. However,\nthe ridge will elongate and keep conditions in the lowlands dry on\nSaturday and Sunday. Temperatures will still run 3-7 degrees above\naverage on both days. As we transition from August to September,\nthe active tropical Pacific will dictate where the upper-level \nridge will be positioned and how much moisture we will see. Both \nthe GFS and the ECMWF have a neat moisture plume just along the \nArizona-New Mexico border for Monday, but the extended model runs\nare in a disagreement as to where the ridge will be stationed. \nThe GFS has the ridge over the Appalachian Mountains and the \nTennessee Valley by Wednesday, and has more moisture over the \nBorderland, whereas the ECMWF has the ridge elongated over us by \nnext Wednesday and keeps moisture levels low. As of now, I am more\nconfident in the ECMWF's prediction, but once the models come \ninto consensus, that could change.",
